      },
      {
        "title": "China's factory dominance",
        "summary": "Shows a Bronson Lot 601 denim jacket, then explains how Chinese factories compete directly with brands through online marketplaces. Contrasts this with brand-label shopping in the US, where shoppers trust ratings and sales volume less than logos.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Value of traditional brands",
        "summary": "Covers the benefits of buying through brands: repairs and warranty, preserving heritage craft, innovation, accountability and brand experience. Also covers the downside of mega-brands whose quality has slipped, as shown by the dupe craze.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 18
      },
      {
        "title": "Risks of factory shopping",
        "summary": "Covers the downsides of buying direct from factories: unreliable ratings, scams, lead contamination, and poor ethical accountability through the supply chain.",
        "first_segment": 19,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Bronson's ethical questions",
        "summary": "Discusses the broken jean button and admits he cannot verify Bronson's labour ethics despite talking with its CEO.",
        "first_segment": 21,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "Global manufacturing shift",
        "summary": "Explains which countries lead low-cost manufacturing and which lead innovation, and why China sits in the middle with a marketplace infrastructure that drives prices down. Gives a final quality verdict on Bronson's OG-107 pants.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 25
      },
      {
        "title": "The future of retail",
        "summary": "Describes US demand for unbranded quality and domestic manufacturing, China becoming more brand-focused, and production moving to Vietnam, Bangladesh and India.",
        "first_segment": 26,
        "last_segment": 27
      }
    ],
